come on if the Call of Duty franchise is  a well-oiled machine the black ops 3 is  the replacement part that keeps the  wheels moving into yet another year it  introduces minor changes to an  established formula and in some aspects  this is developer Treyarch near its feet  but in others call of duty black ops 3  lacks inspirations  Treyarch has set a high bar with its  contributions to the Call of Duty series  but here with call of duty black ops 3  it reaches in several different  directions with vastly different results  the newest iteration of multiplayer  begins on a promising note as black ops  3 specialists cover the screen these are  the soldiers of humanity's future clad  in titanium alloy armor brandishing  multimillion-dollar weapons they're also  black ops 3 s new layer of customization  we still have the traditional loadout  system with 10 slots to spend on weapons  items and equipment but specialists add  a little more nuance with these  characters you're forced to choose  between a power weapon or special  ability as only one can be equipped at a  time and the dichotomy between these two  as well as the possibilities their uses  reveal leads to some dynamic scenarios  from one match to the next certain  powers work well in specific game types  and can shift momentum when used well  and for the first several hours in black  ops 3 s multiplayer I explored as many  possibilities as I could but that sense  of discovery fades with time black ops 3  grants you access to four specialists  out of the gate and subsequent options  unlock in a trickle but there's too much  time between them to keep things moving  along and when I'm not learning the  intricacies of a new character black ops  3 defaults to a more generic Call of  Duty experience this isn't necessarily a  bad thing in fact black ops 3  multiplayer offers some of the best map  designs in the franchise each arena is a  confluence of differing sight lines  hectic clash points and diverse  elevations the new movement system also  creates an action reaction dynamic you  can wall run in to capture points ground  slide out of firefights and clamber over  ledges otherwise out of reach in short  black ops 3 is fluid it just feels good  but it's lack of variety after about 10  hours he races much of the excitement  present at the beginning there's still  the proverbial carrot dangling on a  string in front of us it's just smaller  this time around  your enemy knows Bravo  then there's black ops 3 s new zombies  map shadows of evil it's pure survival  set in a jazzy fictional city complete  with slipspace portals and Cthulhu like  monsters make no mistake this new  version of zombies is bizarre it's also  fantastic there's a looming sense of  mystery as you decide which doors to  unlock next which weapons prove most  effective and what that glowing green  plant even does the difficulty is high  here but zombies now more than ever is a  learning experience and seeing the  tangible results of your practice is  rewarding the undead horde has also  wandered its way into another game mode  it's called nightmares and it unlocks  once you've beaten the campaign  basically Treyarch recycled black ops 3  campaign missions but now with zombies  and a grim voiceover from an unnamed  character and believe it or not this  works there's a slower pace to the  missions here and in reimagining the  story to center around a zombie  infection  Treyarch has created something  magnitudes better than its vanilla  campaign you've been made Hendrix what's  the call  now  Plan B Plan B in contrast to nightmares  the original campaign is a chore it's a  boring crawl that wastes no time  throwing you into its routine you're  thrust into tidal waves of enemies and  fun will be linear pathways on the way  to your next objective there are some  deviations from this pattern but I was  already on autopilot by the fifth  mission settled into a continual routine  of aim shoot reload three-peat there are  fleeting moments when the new cybernetic  modifications changed the way you play  the campaign these abilities let you  control enemy drones stun human  opponents or set fire to robots internal  systems the powers would be more  impactful though if there wasn't such a  lack of enemy variety by and large the  enemy variants just require differing  number of bullets to take down and when  you're using them on such a repetitive  group of targets who react the same way  every time the abilities lose their  novelty black ops 3s narrative doesn't  support the campaign in any way either  it tells an incomprehensible story about  AI ascendancy and the moral greys of a  hyper-connected future raising  intriguing questions but never bothering  to answer them and as it closes the  campaign ends not with a bang but a  whimper in its undead modes and the  first ten hours of multiplayer call of  duty black ops 3 excels but in its  campaign it merely crawls forward black  ops 3 doesn't offer anything remarkable  to the series doing just enough to  maintain the Call of Duty status quo the  franchise however slowly continues its  steady march you could not be here  Taylor Taylor I want to get out of here
 

